# SQL's `<>`: DS0107, with a fix to `!=`.
mode: syntax
---- query
'orders.csv' | where customerId <> "c-001"
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..43
  PIPELINE@0..42
    LITERAL@0..12
      STRING@0..12 "'orders.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@12..13 " "
    PIPE@13..14 "|"
    WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
    WHERE_OP@15..42
      IDENT@15..20 "where"
      WHITESPACE@20..21 " "
      BIN_EXPR@21..42
        NAME_REF@21..31
          IDENT@21..31 "customerId"
        WHITESPACE@31..32 " "
        LTGT@32..34 "<>"
        WHITESPACE@34..35 " "
        LITERAL@35..42
          STRING@35..42 "\"c-001\""
  WHITESPACE@42..43 "\n"

error[DS0107]: `<>` is not an operator
  --> query:1:33
  |
1 | 'orders.csv' | where customerId <> "c-001"
  |                                 ^^ this is SQL's "not equal"
  |
  = help: use `!=` to test that values differ: `customerId != "c-001"`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    'orders.csv' | where customerId != "c-001"
which parses without errors
